g to control play. Playing my Joker and taking the lead would be playing directly into the Landlord\'s hand. This also goes against a core principle: I should *never* beat my teammate\'s high card unless I\'m absolutely certain I can win the game outright, or if it\'s the only way to save them from a certain loss. My teammate played a 2. They made a play! I have to let them continue this initiative.\n\nSo, the optimal strategy here is to pass. This forces the Landlord to make the tough decision: use a resource (Joker or bomb) or concede the lead to my teammate. That\'s pressure I want to apply. I\'m confident in this read, and this play will maximize the advantage for my team.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (Previous Player) has played a 2, which is a very high control card. I currently hold three 2s and the Black Joker. This information confirms that the Landlord (Next Player) holds zero 2s. Consequently, the Landlord cannot beat my teammate\'s 2 with a single card (unless they use the Red Joker) and would otherwise have to use a Bomb. Playing my Black Joker now would waste the team\'s strongest asset to beat my own partner, effectively helping the Landlord. I must let my teammate\'s 2 stand to force out the Landlord\'s Red Joker or Bomb, or allow my teammate to regain the initiative.\n\nReason: Teammate played a 2. I hold the remaining three 2s and the Black Joker. It is strategic suicide to beat a partner\'s high card when the opponent is weak in that rank. Passing forces the Landlord to spend a top-tier resource (Red Joker or Bomb) or pass the turn to my teammate.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
