o.\n\nThe key is that I need to maximize my chances of winning. In Dou Dizhu, you generally don\'t beat your teammate unless you can secure a swift victory or your teammate is signaling for a specific play. Right now, it doesn\'t feel like either of those scenarios is the case. Plus, the person who played 888+3 is my **teammate**.\n\nSo, after careful consideration, I\'m going to pass. It keeps the pressure on the Landlord, preserves my 2s for a potentially better opportunity later, and lets my teammate continue their momentum. It\'s the strategic play here.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current winning hand (888+3) was played by my teammate. I only have a triplet of 2s (222) to beat it. Using my strongest cards (222) to beat my teammate is counter-productive. It wastes my control cards and prevents my teammate from potentially winning the round or forcing the Landlord to spend high cards. I should save my 2s to beat the Landlord if they try to top my teammate\'s hand, or to control the game later.\n\nReason: My teammate is currently in control of the table. I should not fight my teammate. Passing allows the teammate to continue if the Landlord cannot respond, or forces the Landlord to play high cards to beat the teammate, which I can then decide to beat with my 222 later.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
